After 17 hours awake, reaction time and decision accuracy decline to the equivalent of a blood-alcohol concentration of 0.05% — legally intoxicated in many countries.
The prefrontal cortex — responsible for judgement, impulse control, and complex reasoning — is the first brain region to degrade under sleep deprivation, impairing the exact skills most needed for good decisions.
Chronic sleep restriction to 6 hours per night for two weeks produces cognitive deficits equivalent to two full nights of total sleep deprivation, yet subjects report feeling only 'slightly sleepy.'
The US CDC estimates drowsy driving causes 72,000 crashes, 44,000 injuries, and 800 deaths annually — more than alcohol and drugs combined in some age groups.
